{"motion":"This House would ban advertising for environmentally harmful products.","debate_id":"mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::0::3160","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::0::3161","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::3162","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::3163","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::2::3164","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::2::3165","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::3::3166","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::3::3167","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::4::3168","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::4::3169","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::3170","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::3171","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3172","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3173","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3174","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3175","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3176","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3177","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3178","multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3179"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::0::3160","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Banning advertising for environmentally harmful products is a moral imperative because it prevents the promotion of behaviors that knowingly degrade the planet and harm future generations. Advertising shapes consumer choices and normalizes consumption patterns; endorsing products that damage the environment contradicts our ethical responsibility to protect shared ecological resources. Allowing such ads prioritizes profit over planetary well-being, which is fundamentally unjust."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::0::3161","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral responsibility to protect the environment does not justify banning advertising, as it infringes on the ethical principle of free expression and informed choice. Consumers have a right to access information about legal products, even if those products carry environmental costs. Suppressing advertising shifts moral agency away from individuals and toward the state, undermining personal accountability. A more ethical approach is to ensure transparency and education, not censorship."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::3162","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Banning advertising for environmentally harmful products addresses social inequities, as the negative effects of environmental degradation disproportionately impact marginalized communities. Advertising normalizes consumption patterns that sustain these harms, reinforcing systemic injustices. Limiting such ads helps shift social norms toward more sustainable and equitable behaviors, reducing the burden on vulnerable populations."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::3163","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"While the Pro side highlights social inequities, banning advertising for environmentally harmful products risks exacerbating social divides by limiting access to information about legal goods, particularly for lower-income consumers who rely on advertised options to make cost-effective choices. Such a ban may also stigmatize certain communities that depend on these products due to economic constraints, without addressing the root causes of environmental harm. Social progress is better achieved through inclusive education and equitable access to sustainable alternatives, rather than through restrictive measures that may deepen existing disparities."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::2::3164","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Banning advertising for environmentally harmful products creates economic incentives for businesses to innovate and shift toward sustainable alternatives, aligning market signals with long-term environmental health. This policy reduces the externalized costs of pollution and resource depletion by discouraging demand for high-impact goods, thereby lowering future public expenditures on environmental remediation and public health. Over time, it fosters a more resilient and adaptive economy less dependent on ecologically destructive industries."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::2::3165","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that an advertising ban would spur green innovation ignores the immediate economic disruption it would cause, particularly for small businesses and industries reliant on currently legal products. Such a ban would reduce consumer demand abruptly, leading to job losses and decreased tax revenues without guaranteeing a smooth transition to sustainable alternatives. Market shifts are more effectively driven by subsidies and incentives for green products rather than punitive restrictions on information, which can distort competition and entrench advantages for larger firms better able to absorb regulatory costs."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::3::3166","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"From a legal perspective, the state has a legitimate interest in regulating commercial speech that promotes activities causing significant public harm, as established in precedents allowing restrictions on advertising for tobacco and alcohol. Environmentally harmful products contribute to measurable public costs, including climate damage and health impacts, justifying similar regulatory treatment. Such a ban would be content-neutral in targeting only commercial messages tied to demonstrable harm, and would likely withstand intermediate scrutiny under existing free speech doctrines. Therefore, the proposed ban aligns with established legal principles governing the limits of commercial advertising."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::3::3167","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s legal argument overlooks that commercial speech protections apply broadly to all legal products, not just those deemed harmful in hindsight. Unlike tobacco or alcohol, which are subject to specific statutory frameworks, most environmentally harmful products are fully legal and regulated under existing environmental laws, making a blanket advertising ban legally overbroad. Such a ban would likely fail intermediate scrutiny because it restricts more speech than necessary when less restrictive alternatives, like mandatory environmental disclosures, are available. Therefore, the proposed ban exceeds the state\u2019s legitimate regulatory authority under current free speech jurisprudence."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::4::3168","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"A ban on advertising environmentally harmful products strengthens the state\u2019s ability to align policy with climate commitments and public mandates for environmental action. Politically, it signals a clear prioritization of long-term ecological security over short-term commercial interests, reinforcing governmental legitimacy in the face of growing public demand for climate accountability. Such a measure also reduces the influence of corporate lobbying that often obstructs stronger environmental regulations, thereby rebalancing political power toward democratic sustainability goals."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::4::3169","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that an advertising ban would reduce corporate lobbying influence ignores the political reality that such bans often provoke stronger industry backlash and increased lobbying efforts to overturn or weaken the policy. Moreover, restricting advertising shifts political focus away from comprehensive regulatory solutions\u2014like carbon pricing or production standards\u2014toward symbolic measures that may appease public demand without delivering substantive environmental outcomes. This approach risks undermining democratic deliberation by substituting speech restrictions for transparent, inclusive policymaking on complex environmental challenges."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::3170","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"From a technological standpoint, banning advertising for environmentally harmful products accelerates the adoption of cleaner technologies by redirecting market demand and investment toward sustainable innovations. Advertising shapes consumer awareness and preferences, and its removal for high-impact products reduces the economic viability of outdated, polluting technologies. This creates a clearer market signal for firms to prioritize research and development in low-carbon alternatives, fostering faster technological transitions aligned with climate goals."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::3171","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side\u2019s technological argument assumes that removing advertising directly accelerates clean tech adoption, but it ignores how advertising funds the R&D cycles for transitional technologies, even in high-emission sectors. Many environmentally harmful products are currently necessary for the infrastructure that supports emerging green technologies, such as rare earth mining for batteries or fossil-fueled grid backup for renewable intermittency. Banning their advertising could stifle the very innovation pathways needed to achieve sustainability by cutting off market feedback and revenue streams that enable technological iteration."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3172","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"The legal framework for regulating commercial speech permits restrictions when they serve a substantial government interest and are narrowly tailored. Banning advertising for environmentally harmful products meets this standard, as it directly advances the state\u2019s interest in mitigating environmental harm and public health costs. Unlike less targeted measures, this ban focuses on promotional content that actively encourages consumption of high-impact goods, making it a proportionate legal response consistent with existing regulatory precedents."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3173","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Con side\u2019s claim that the ban is overbroad ignores that the regulation targets only commercial speech promoting products with demonstrable, significant environmental harm, not all legal goods. Existing legal doctrine permits such tailored restrictions when they serve a substantial government interest and leave open ample alternative channels for communication. Mandatory disclosures alone are insufficient when advertising actively drives demand for harmful products, making a ban a necessary and proportionate legal measure."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3174","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"The economic argument against the ban underestimates the long-term fiscal benefits of reducing demand for environmentally harmful products. By curbing advertising, the policy lowers the societal costs of pollution, healthcare, and climate adaptation that currently burden public budgets. It also levels the playing field for green businesses by removing the artificial demand boost that advertising gives to polluting industries, fostering a more efficient and sustainable market allocation of resources."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3175","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s latest economic argument overlooks the immediate fiscal instability such a ban would create for industries and workers dependent on currently legal products. Abrupt demand suppression without guaranteed market readiness for alternatives risks economic dislocation that could outweigh long-term savings. Moreover, the claim that the ban levels the playing field ignores how larger firms can more easily adapt to advertising restrictions, potentially increasing market concentration rather than fostering fair competition."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3176","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral case for banning advertising of environmentally harmful products rests on the duty to prevent foreseeable harm. By promoting products that degrade the environment, advertisers contribute to systemic damage that violates the rights of future generations and vulnerable populations. Choosing to restrict such advertising is not censorship but a necessary act of moral responsibility to align societal behavior with ecological justice."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3177","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Con side\u2019s earlier moral argument that banning advertising undermines personal accountability fails to recognize that moral responsibility includes preventing harm when one has the power to do so. Advertising actively shapes desires and normalizes consumption, making it a vector of collective harm rather than a neutral conduit of information. Preserving unfettered advertising for environmentally destructive products prioritizes abstract notions of choice over concrete duties to protect the vulnerable and the planet. True moral agency requires structuring choices within ethically sustainable boundaries, not leaving individuals to navigate a landscape skewed by profit-driven promotion of harm."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3178","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Banning advertising for environmentally harmful products supports social cohesion by reducing the normalization of consumption patterns that harm shared environments, particularly in communities already burdened by pollution and climate impacts. It helps shift cultural norms toward collective responsibility and sustainability, rather than reinforcing individualistic consumption driven by commercial messaging. This policy also limits the social pressure on low-income groups to purchase harmful products due to pervasive advertising, promoting more equitable behavioral norms."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_031_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3179","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s latest social argument assumes that banning advertising will reduce social pressure on low-income groups, but it neglects how such a ban may limit their access to information about affordable, legal products. Without addressing structural inequities in access to sustainable alternatives, the policy risks imposing middle-class environmental norms on communities that lack viable substitutes. This could deepen social fragmentation rather than foster cohesion, as it stigmatizes necessary consumption without providing equitable pathways to change."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.9,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.58,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.57,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.75],[0.9,0.0,0.66,0.03,0.45,0.05,0.82,0.05,0.58,0.05,0.47,0.0,0.78,0.86,0.32,0.0,0.95,0.92,0.66,0.05],[0.0,0.88,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.4,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.05,0.02,0.45,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.85],[0.82,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.35,0.07,0.3,0.08,0.26,0.03,0.28,0.06,0.4,0.7,0.3,0.15,0.6,0.56,0.9,0.0],[0.0,0.75,0.0,0.62,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.52],[0.4,0.0,0.2,0.02,0.78,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.45,0.05,0.8,0.05,0.28,0.56,0.82,0.02,0.42,0.05,0.2,0.05],[0.0,0.6,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.3],[0.22,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.2,0.15,0.08,0.1,0.88,0.9,0.12,0.05,0.2,0.08,0.08,0.08],[0.0,0.85,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.56],[0.32,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.3,0.12,0.32,0.1,0.9,0.0,0.3,0.05,0.6,0.45,0.22,0.08,0.42,0.28,0.28,0.05],